# Add an AIS NIXL backend: hipFile's asynchronous stream API, alongside the
# existing thread-pool AIS_MT.
#
# This mirrors upstream's pairing on the NVIDIA side -- GDS (submission-based)
# next to GDS_MT (thread pool) -- so the plugin, its files and its classes carry
# the same names with GDS/gds swapped for AIS/ais, and it reuses the existing
# rocm_ais_path / disable_rocm_ais_backend meson options rather than adding new
# ones.  AIS_MT is untouched: it is the baseline this is measured against.
#
# What it is NOT is a port of cuda_gds's batch path, which was the obvious
# structural choice.  hipFile's batch API is a stub as of develop bd0bc233:
# BatchContext::submit_operations only validates the params and parks them in an
# outstanding_ops set -- no I/O is ever issued -- and hipFileBatchIOGetStatus,
# hipFileBatchIOCancel and hipFileBatchIODestroy all throw
# std::runtime_error("Not Implemented").  A batch port would submit cleanly,
# move zero bytes, and then fail in checkXfer.  The async path is real:
# hipFileReadAsync/WriteAsync reach Backend::async_io, implemented by both the
# fastpath and fallback backends with an AsyncMonitor completion thread.
#
# So the engine is structured around HIP streams instead of batch handles:
#
#   - a stream is the unit of submission.  Ops on one stream complete in order;
#     ops on different streams overlap.  batch_limit therefore means "requests
#     chained onto one stream" and batch_pool_size means "streams held per
#     device" -- the parameter keys keep cuda_gds's names so that one nixlbench
#     sweep row (--gds_batch_pool_size / --gds_batch_limit) configures either
#     backend.
#   - the pool is keyed by device, not a single free list, because
#     Fastpath::async_io rejects a buffer whose GPU id differs from the
#     stream's.  Streams are created on first use: no device is known at
#     construction time.
#   - exhausting the pool chains extra requests onto streams already taken
#     rather than failing the transfer, which is what cuda_gds does on
#     "batch pool exhausted".
#   - hipFileReadAsync takes the size and both offsets by pointer and reads them
#     when the op reaches the head of the stream, not at submission, so those
#     fields live in the request handle and releaseReqH synchronises before
#     letting it die.
#   - completion status is the per-request byte count and nothing else: hipFile
#     reports a POSIX errno as a negative count and clamps one op to
#     getMaxRwCount(), neither of which surfaces through the stream.  checkXfer
#     treats a short transfer as an error, which the cuFile original does not --
#     nixlGdsIOBatch::checkStatus counts returned events and ignores their
#     status field entirely.
#
# The meson gate tests for hipFileReadAsync with has_header_symbol and calls
# subdir_done() when it is absent, so an older hipFile drops the plugin instead
# of breaking the build -- the same shape as the existing hipFile-not-found
# path that AIS_MT already uses.

